1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,996
Welcome to the evocative
Hall of Candelabras,
so named because the
speleothems found in this
section of the route
resemble, in shape and
colour, elegant

2
00:00:10,996 --> 00:00:23,656
nineteenth-century
candelabras, with wax
seemingly dripping slowly
from the candles. We are now
in the first of the four
halls we will visit along
a route of approximately one

3
00:00:23,656 --> 00:00:35,882
kilometre. In reality,
however, the Grotta del Bue
Marino is part of a much
larger karst system. The
cave you are visiting today
connects, through
underground galleries,

4
00:00:35,882 --> 00:00:49,338
to three other cavities: the
Grotta di Su Palu, the
Grotta di Su Molente, and
the Grotta di Monte Longos.
There are therefore four
external entrances leading
to a single vast underground

5
00:00:49,338 --> 00:01:01,636
system, with approximately
75 kilometres explored
so far. It is important
to stress this: the system
is still being explored.
We do not yet know its true
extent. With its

6
00:01:01,636 --> 00:01:14,441
75 kilometres already
documented, it is currently
the second longest karst
system in Italy. The Grotta
del Bue Marino is the only
part of the system
accessible to the public and

7
00:01:14,441 --> 00:01:26,305
is divided into three
branches: the northern
branch, approximately 9
km long, currently closed
to the public; the central
branch, 5 km long,
accessible only to cave

8
00:01:26,305 --> 00:01:40,267
divers; the southern branch,
8 km long, the one we are
visiting today, of which
only the first kilometre has
been open to visitors since
1983, the year of its first
public opening. The southern

9
00:01:40,267 --> 00:01:52,203
branch is still active.
Along the route you will
notice dripping water,
a sign that the formations
are still slowly developing:
the cave is constantly
changing, even

10
00:01:52,203 --> 00:02:04,284
if imperceptibly to our
eyes. But the cave also
changes through another, far
more powerful force: the
erosive action of
an underground river that
still flows today and

11
00:02:04,284 --> 00:02:17,595
continues to carve out these
galleries. For almost the
entire length of our route,
there is seawater beneath
our feet. At the end of the
route, we will instead
encounter an underground

12
00:02:17,595 --> 00:02:31,195
river: ahead of us will flow
fresh, clear, and silent
water. It is precisely this
watercourse that is the
principal architect of the
entire karst system. Through
its erosive force, exerted

13
00:02:31,195 --> 00:02:42,842
over millions of years,
it has carved through the
limestone, shaping
galleries, widening
cavities, and contributing
to the formation of most
of the 75 kilometres

14
00:02:42,842 --> 00:02:55,719
explored to date. As you
walk through these halls,
try to imagine a time when
the spaces you see were
completely submerged. During
the early stages of its
formation, the cave was

15
00:02:55,719 --> 00:03:08,306
filled with fresh water
right up to the ceiling. The
mighty underground river
occupied every space,
exerting a continuous
erosive action on the rock
in its slow and ceaseless

16
00:03:08,306 --> 00:03:20,966
journey in search of
a natural outlet. That
outlet was found at the sea:
the current entrance to the
Grotta del Bue Marino
coincides in fact with the
mouth of that underground

17
00:03:20,966 --> 00:03:34,566
river, the terminal stretch
of a vast system shaped
by water over geological
time. When the river opened
its outlet to the sea, the
water level inside the cave
gradually dropped. The halls

18
00:03:34,566 --> 00:03:47,226
emptied and the sea became
part of the underground
environment. Only then, drop
by drop, in silence and
darkness, did all the
formations you see today
begin to slowly grow and

19
00:03:47,226 --> 00:04:00,320
take shape. What we are
observing is not simply
a cave, but the result of
a long and uninterrupted
dialogue between water and
stone: a natural work of art
shaped by the silent force

20
00:04:00,320 --> 00:04:03,720
of a river hidden in the
heart of the mountain.
